{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790009526,"nonce":"BzxutCMxJ/3DII1EFFOmteaHIA4=","title":"Decision: where automation runs, given no daemon","message":"AGENTS.md rules out a daemon (d35de2e, d591cb3, 63c68d1 settled concurrency without one), but a rule engine is defined by acting when something happens. So where does evaluation live?\n\n(a) Opportunistically, at the end of every mutating command. No new process, and the common case (I moved an issue, a rule fires) is immediate. But a change arriving by pull or Jira sync with nobody running a command sits unprocessed, and short CLI calls now do unbounded extra work.\n(b) In long-lived processes — webui and the rewritten TUI — driven by the ref watcher from 63c68d1. Reacts to other people's writes and to sync, which (a) cannot. But rules only fire while somebody has a UI open, which is not a guarantee you can build on.\n(c) On a schedule, in CI or cron, over the op log since a watermark. The only option with a real guarantee, at the cost of latency and of somewhere to run it.\n\nProbably (a) and (b) for responsiveness with (c) as the backstop that makes it correct — the same shape as the watcher's fsnotify-plus-poll.\n\nWhatever wins, two properties are forced: actions must be idempotent, because more than one of these can fire for the same change; and progress is a watermark over the op log, not a done-flag per rule, because ops arrive out of order and eventually.","files":null},{"type":9,"timestamp":1790009526,"nonce":"lp7FTA3XTNwXcl/CU/hfeOxcZFk=","key":"type","value":"decision"},{"type":9,"timestamp":1790009526,"nonce":"Sfsp9A4MwjrEks9y1HyCIiRu+3w=","key":"status","value":"to-do"}]}